A||||16||Art Doyle||Future Fiero parts availability?||||5||
Z||000000||Art Doyle||11-13-2001||08:57 PM||||I had my first run in with this when I learned that the OEM clutch is no longer available. What is the prognosis for parts availability in the future?<P>The Fiero is a very simple car....strong frame and minimal fluff. Surely it can be kept on the road for many years?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula88||11-13-2001||09:33 PM||||Well, the OEM clutch may not be available, but there are multitudes of clutches that are available.  I'm currently running the RAM clutch from The Fiero Store.  They're overpriced, but they have factory GM parts you can't find anywhere else.  Rodney Dickman makes good equipment.  He's a great source for replacement or alternative parts that GM doesn't make anymore.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tryxalon||11-13-2001||10:01 PM||||Fiero Store Overpriced? <P> Well, maybe when compared to equivilent prices for a current production vehicle for stock or good quality replacement parts with the entire power of the Aftermarket behind you. <P>They are still cheaper than Dealer on Many parts.  <P>Guess it is what you expect.  I've found the service to be excellent, availablity to be good and the quality high so I don't agree.<P>(no I don't work for them, but you have to remember most cars out of production for 15 years have little or NO source of parts!) <P> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||86 FIERO GT||11-13-2001||10:06 PM||||I dunno about pontiac places around yall, but mine don't like fieros.  Alot of the parts are already discontinued already, like all the GT round moldings for the doors and stuff around the car are all discon.  Most of the interior is disontinued too.<P>------------------<BR>If I can't beat the Civic next to me, Ill be polite and flip him off as he passes me.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mickey_Moose||11-13-2001||10:32 PM||||well, it depends on the company, and what car it was for. GM is still good to get parts from for a 68 pick-up - but that is only because they probably have bins of this stuff just laying around, due to the number of trucks made back then. Mopar is also really good when it comes to thier 'muscle car' line.<P>I think the biggest problem with the Fiero was the limited production line...<P>just my 2 cents<P>Tim<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Loki||11-13-2001||10:49 PM||||Hey Art. What part of Bedford?  Im right off Central and Harwood....<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/fieroloki/lokisig1%20copy.jpg">  <BR>"All Fieros broke right now" :(||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierobruiser||11-14-2001||12:19 AM||||The Fiero Store is always a good source for parts as well as Rodney. Try this site for way better prices than your local GM dealer.<BR>It helps a lot if you have a P22 or access to Fiero P/N's. <A HREF="http://gmpartsdirect.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://gmpartsdirect.com/</A> <P>------------------<BR>Notchbacks Rule<BR>85GT(show car)<BR>88Coupe (daily driver)<BR>84 Coupe(project car)||||reg||1
Z||000007||camon||11-14-2001||12:37 AM||||I too think that the Fiero store charges too much for parts. Doubt I will ever buy anything from them because for the most part anything they sell you can get from your local Autozone, Advance auto parts, napa or Pepboys stores and it will not cost you as much geting it at one of these places compared to the fiero store.<BR>Granted the fiero store sells some things that you can't get just anywhere....but if you compare the stuff they do carry that other places have you will see a big difference in price<P>example:<BR>I bought an accel coil for 40.00 and fiero store wants 60.00 for the same identical coil (what a 20.00 differnce and then yet throw shipping on top of that) No Thank You!<P>Nope i'll stick with local auto part stores unless I need something I can only get there. Hopefull it never comes down to that!<P>------------------<BR>Keith<p>[This message has been edited by camon (edited 11-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||DXR_DAD||11-14-2001||01:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mickey_Moose:<BR><B>well, it depends on the company, and what car it was for. GM is still good to get parts from for a 68 pick-up - but that is only because they probably have bins of this stuff just laying around, due to the number of trucks made back then. Mopar is also really good when it comes to thier 'muscle car' line.<P>I think the biggest problem with the Fiero was the limited production line...<P>just my 2 cents<P>Tim</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Your right Tim, I never have a problem getting parts for my 68,69 Camaros? Or my 65,66,72,Chevelles!<BR>The limited production of the fiero was it.<P>and there seems to a lot of after market company in the 65-72 Muscle car field  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>My kinda car!!!!!<P>DXR_DAD<P>||||reg||14
Z||000009||neohio||11-14-2001||11:44 AM||||The Fiero Store was the only place in the world that had a upper control arm bolt..I looked EVERYWHERE and no luck what-so-ever..sure, the part was more than I was willing to pay for it but when your in a bind and its the only place that carries it i guess you'll spend the extra money..they were really nice and I recieved it in 2 days..I will do business with them in the future for parts that are non-exsistent..||||reg||6
Z||000010||JohnF||11-14-2001||11:54 AM||||Hey Art, <P>Welcome to PFF!  However, you must post a pix of your Fiero, Heheheh!<P>I, like Loki, am located nearby.<P>As to your problem with Pontiac, the warehouses have simply run out of extra parts and currently aren't builing more.  That seems as if it might change in the future if GM makes the decision to classify our passion as classics and releases the engineering and jigs, etc. to 3rd parties for builing new parts.  <P>In the meantime, we have a lot of 3rd party supplier and manufacturers, just look around.||||reg||12
Z||000011||Fformula88||11-14-2001||03:44 PM||||Future parts availability really depends on the parts.  Most of the mechanical stuff is shared with other GM cars, so it should continue to be readily available (engine, transmissions, brakes 'cept for 88's).  <P>What I am concerned most about is the availability of suspension parts for my 88 Formula.  I have a pair of brand new front wheel bearing hubs salted away that I bought when they were discontinued so I am covered there... but I am having a lot of trouble finding rear springs.  I am deciding on whether I want to go with lowering springs, used ones or have a pair custom made ($$).<P>I know some people do not like Fiero Store for various reasons.  Some of their stuff is pricey.  However, I try to give them a lot of support because its the places that specialize in Fiero's that will keep parts available, and have new ones fabricated when old ones disappear.  But they need to have the business to get companies to make low volumes of parts.  Same goes for others too... Rodney Dickman, Bob Steiger etc etc.<P>I think the Fiero community is strong enough that there will at least be good sources of quality used parts (like from Kick Hill farms) to keep the Fieros we own on the road for a long time, even if some new parts disappear.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Art Doyle||11-20-2001||11:42 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierobruiser:<BR><B>The Fiero Store is always a good source for parts as well as Rodney. Try this site for way better prices than your local GM dealer.<BR>It helps a lot if you have a P22 or access to Fiero P/N's. <A HREF="http://gmpartsdirect.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://gmpartsdirect.com/</A> <P><BR>My favorite parts store. Best damn GM source there is. They get *all* my money when they can supply the goods.<P>I have the Fiero microfiche....now if I can only snag a viewer (grin).<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Art Doyle||11-20-2001||11:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B>Hey Art, <P>Welcome to PFF!  However, you must post a pix of your Fiero, Heheheh!<P>I, like Loki, am located nearby.<P>As to your problem with Pontiac, the warehouses have simply run out of extra parts and currently aren't builing more.  That seems as if it might change in the future if GM makes the decision to classify our passion as classics and releases the engineering and jigs, etc. to 3rd parties for builing new parts.  <P>In the meantime, we have a lot of 3rd party supplier and manufacturers, just look around.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What are the chances of this happening?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Art Doyle||11-20-2001||11:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B>Hey Art, <P>Welcome to PFF!  However, you must post a pix of your Fiero, Heheheh!<P>.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's my teenaged son's car, and we feel very secure entrusting him to the Fiero's safety cage. It also provides him with a little upkeep struggle (which is good for affluenza avoidance). Most of the kids over here hold their nose when they see it, but the work this kid is putting into it will make this Fiero *his* for a long time. <P>||||reg||1
